harness: jxl-encoder/examples/distance_targeting_probe (RESAMPLING unused here)
analysis: scripts/qfseed_lift_ab_analyze.py
commit: see git log for benchmarks/qfseed_lift_ab_2026-09-06.tsv
question: (a) what explains the ONE-SHOT (e5-e7, no perceptual loop) behaviour of
  the qf-seed lift, and (b) does the lift ever pay on the RD curve?
gates A/B'd (two, not one — both must be set to turn the lift off):
  e in [5,7]: JXL_W44_109_ADAPTIVE_QUANT_QF_SCALE=1.0  (defaults 2.0 e5/e6, 3.0 e7)
  e >= 8    : JXL_BUTTLOOP_INITIAL_QF_SCALE=1.0        (default 4.0)
grid: 4 images x efforts {5,7,8} x mode {on,off};
  on  distances 2,3,3.4,3.6,4,5,6,8
  off distances 0.75,1,1.25,1.5,2,2.5,3,3.4,3.6,4,5,6,8 (finer, to bracket the
  lifted points' delivered quality for the matched-quality comparison)
images: gb82-sc terminal.png + codec_wiki.png (true screenshots, the W44-105
  calibration content), imazen-26 9291 beard-oil (ai-products), 7026 line-00081
  (plots). All centre-cropped to <=1024 per axis.
metrics: jxl-oxide srgb_linear decode; butteraugli_linear (default params) at
  FULL resolution; fast_ssim2 compute_ssimulacra2 on sRGB u8.
matched-quality rule: cheapest MEASURED unlifted setting whose butteraugli is
  <= the lifted point's. Conservative in the lift's FAVOUR when the unlifted
  grid is coarse (the qualifying point may overshoot quality, inflating its
  bytes), so the lift's measured wins are an upper bound.
CAVEAT: 4 images, 33 firing cells. Direction (win/loss counts) is more robust
  than the absolute cost numbers. Not sufficient to re-calibrate W44-105/107/108.
